# A python primop that closes over its state leaks the state

**DONE.** Opened as "a leak report rides every suite run", diagnosed
as an uncollectable reference cycle, and fixed by teaching the class
to be traversed. Zero leaked instances now, where there were four.

## What the suite said

    nanobind: leaked 4 instances!
     - leaked instance ... of type "huggorm_bindings.eval.EvalState"  (x4)
    nanobind: leaked 1 types!
    nanobind: leaked 18 functions!

The type and its functions leaked BECAUSE the instances did: nanobind
cannot free a type while an instance of it lives.

Not `tasks/089`, and measured: the same four appeared with that
task's new tests deselected.

## Which four, and the discriminator

Bisected by file, then by test. Exactly four tests, one each:

    test_primop::test_a_python_function_answers_as_a_builtin
    test_primop::test_the_arguments_arrive_forced
    test_function::test_python_calls_nix_calling_python
    test_function::test_a_python_primop_applied_through_a_lambda

Forty other tests in those files leaked nothing, and four of THOSE
also call `register_primop`. So it was never registration:

    leaks      lambda a, b: state.make_int(a.integer() + b.integer())
    leaks      def peek(v): ...; return state.make_int(...)
    clean      lambda v: v
    clean      lambda v: 42
    clean      def boom(v): raise ValueError(...)

Every leaking callable names `state`. Every clean one does not.

## The cycle

`register_primop` captured the callable BY VALUE as an `nb::object`
inside the lambda it stored in the state's base environment. So:

    Python EvalState wrapper
      -> Evaluator -> shared_ptr<EvalCore> -> nix::EvalState
      -> base env -> nix::PrimOp -> impl lambda
      -> captured nb::object -> the closure's cell
      -> Python EvalState wrapper

The `weak_ptr<EvalCore>` already in that lambda breaks the C++ arm and
was put there for this reason. It cannot help: the arm that CLOSES the
cycle runs from C++ memory into Python, and Python's collector cannot
follow it.

Two `gc.collect()` calls did not free it. Uncollectable, not merely
uncollected.

## Why it mattered

Closing over the state is the NATURAL way to write a primop - the
result has to come from `state.make_int` - so the useful callables
were exactly the leaking ones. The clean ones in the suite are clean
because they return their argument or raise.

For a service meant to run for days, such a state could never be
reclaimed, and it holds a store, its GC roots and its thread
registration.

## One probe that lied

An early probe did `del state` before checking and read "no leak" as
"no cycle". The lambda closes over that same binding through a CELL,
so `del` does not drop a reference - it EMPTIES the cell and breaks
the cycle by hand. The probe was undoing the thing it measured, and
the wrong reading was believed for two runs.

`del` looks like a neutral way to drop a reference. It is not, when a
closure shares the binding.

## The fix

**The core owns the callable; the lambda carries an index.** One
strong Python reference, in one place a GC slot can report and drop.
`release` empties each slot rather than the vector, because an index
handed to a lambda has to stay valid.

**Two CPython slots, and a DSL knob that names them.** nanobind offers
no abstraction - its own `refleaks.rst` says to drop to the slots -
so `evaluator_tp_traverse` and `evaluator_tp_clear` are hand-written
in `cpp/eval.hpp`, and `evaluator_slots` is the table. The declaration
says `@gc_slots("huggorm::evaluator_slots")` and the emitted
`nb::class_` passes `nb::type_slots(...)`.

That is the helper shape Carl described on the same day: manual C++
the EMITTER BINDS TO. Nothing here resolves a Python name, and
generated code is the only caller.

**A destructor that drops the callables with the GIL held.** An
`nb::object` going out of scope decrements a Python refcount, and the
core's deleter can run on a pool worker or the server's reaper -
`make_core` exists because of exactly that. A destructor body also
runs before any member is destroyed, which removes the member-order
question.

## Measured

    before      4 leaked instances, 1 type, 18 functions
    after       0
    suite       408 passed

## The gate, and the half it does NOT cover

`test_a_primop_closing_over_its_state_does_not_leak_it` uses a CANARY
in the closure, not a weakref: the bound type has no weakref slot
("cannot create weak reference to
'huggorm_bindings.eval.EvalState'"). The canary dies exactly when the
closure does. It calls no `del`, for the reason above.

Proved by breaking it. `evaluator_tp_traverse` reporting nothing:

    1 failed, 397 passed, 10 deselected
    - leaked instance ... EvalState   (x4, the originals back)

**`tp_clear` is NOT covered, and that was measured rather than
assumed.** Making it release nothing changes nothing - 408 passed,
zero leaks. CPython needs `tp_traverse` on every type in a cycle and
`tp_clear` on only ONE of them, and the other participants here are a
function object and a cell, which carry their own.

It stays anyway. That is a fact about THIS cycle, not about the class:
an `EvalState` reached only through C++ participants would have
nothing else to clear. Kept as correctness, recorded as untested.

## What is left, and it is not this file

Nothing detects a class that stores a Python object and omits
`@gc_slots`. Today there is one such class and it says it. `tasks/094`
holds the check.

Opened 2026-09-05, from a suite run for `tasks/089`. Closed the same
day.

## The invariant the fix leans on, checked rather than assumed

`primops_` takes no lock. It is WRITTEN on the state's affine thread
and READ by `tp_traverse` on whichever thread runs a collection, so a
`push_back` racing an iteration would reallocate under it.

What makes that safe is the GIL, and the one link worth checking is
`hold_primop`. The emitted `register_primop` binding takes NO
`nb::call_guard<nb::gil_scoped_release>` - read in the emitted
`eval.cpp`, where `subscribe_logs` two lines below it DOES take one.
So registration runs with the GIL held, as does every other reader.

Written into the header, because a future `blocking` change or a
release guard added for tidiness would turn it into a data race with
nothing to say so.

## The docstring that had become false

`register_primop`'s declaration said the leak was PERMANENT and told
a caller to "capture what the callable needs and not the state". That
is not advice anybody can take - a primop builds its result with
`state.make_int` - and it is now wrong as well.

It was true and measured when written. Corrected in the same commit
rather than left, because a declaration documenting the opposite of
what happens is the shape `tasks/090` found and `CLAUDE.md` names.
